module gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.gate_closure_narrower_than_the_closure_its_merge_governs

import std.types { NonEmptyStr }
import gunbc.recurring_failure_mode { RecurringFailureMode }

data gate_closure_narrower_than_the_closure_its_merge_governs: RecurringFailureMode = RecurringFailureMode {
  identity: "gate_closure_narrower_than_the_closure_its_merge_governs" as NonEmptyStr,

  receipts: [
    "**a change is admitted on a gate that compiles ONE repository, and the tree the merge actually governs spans TWO, because a second repository builds against this one's main.** INVALID STATE: a merge admitted on a gate whose compiled closure is a strict subset of the closure its result governs. The coupling is directional and asymmetric: repository A's gate compiles A alone; repository B's gate compiles A+B, but it only ever runs for changes authored in B. So a change authored in A can be green by every gate that will ever see it and still break B's main, and NOTHING compiled the two together before it landed. ",

    "SPECIMEN, 2026-09-11, COMPLETE. Public gunbc#11057, \"Refuse a zero-field construction of a fields-declaring type\", landed green on public CI. It made a bare `ExitFailure` fail to RESOLVE -- `std.process` declares `ExitFailure { code, reason }`, and refusing the zero-field construction of a fields-declaring type is exactly what that PR was for, so the public change was correct and its gate was right to admit it. ELEVEN witnesses in gunbc-private used bare `ExitFailure` as their failure arm: `asset_lifecycle`, `buyers_pipeline`, `ci_sellable_unit`, `mobo_x_architecture`, `mobo_x_memory_census`, `panel_ruling_procedure`, `pareto_comparison_procedure`, `pricing_decomposition`, `rack_power_claims`, `site_power_survey`, `workflow_stage_gate`. gunbc-private CI builds against public main, so `private_roster_main` stopped resolving ENTIRELY -- 0 PASS, not a partial failure -- and every private PR plus private main went red at once. Repaired by hand in gunbc-private#82: `exit_failure(reason: ...)` plus the import at all eleven sites, roster back to 33/33. ",

    "THE DETECTION SHAPE IS PART OF THE CLASS AND IS WHY THE FIRST READING WAS WRONG. The break was TOTAL rather than partial -- a module every witness resolves through stopped resolving, so the count went to zero rather than down -- and a zero reads as INFRASTRUCTURE, not as semantics. The first plausible hypothesis was \"private CI is broken\", which is a hypothesis about the runner, the checkout or the binary; the true reading, \"a public change removed a construction we depend on\", is not reachable from anything in B's own history. B's authors bisect against a change they did not make and cannot see. A PARTIAL failure would have pointed at the eleven sites and named the construction; totality is what destroys the pointer. ",

    "HARM: the two signals are asymmetrically wrong in opposite directions. A's authors get NO signal at all -- their gate is green and stays green, so the cost they caused is invisible to them and never ranks (DESIGN section 5, externalization: a cost-causing actor leaving a caused cost unpriced onto another principal). B's authors get a signal that names none of the cause: a total red whose located diagnostics are all in B, about declarations B did not change, caused by a commit in a repository B's history does not contain. Every author-hour spent is spent by the side that did nothing. ",

    "RECOGNITION RULE: at a landing, compare the closure the gate COMPILED with the closure the merged result GOVERNS. The first is a fact about the workflow's source roots; the second is the set of repositories that build against the merged branch. Where the second is larger, every green on that gate is evidence about a part. The tell in the wild is directional and cheap to check: a repository that pins ANOTHER repository's branch (not a release, a branch) and whose upstream's workflow does not name it. ",

    "DISTINCT FROM THE SAME-REPO JOINTLY-INCOMPATIBLE CLASS, which is `gunbc.plans.ci_merge_freshness` (the class and its ceiling; gunbc#10984 recorded instance 1) and `gunbc.plans.joint_incompatibility_claim_join` (gunbc#11044, which scopes a mitigation beneath that ceiling). There, TWO changes in ONE repository are each green and their union compiles nowhere; the deficit is FRESHNESS -- each run compiled the right closure at the wrong time -- and a merge queue is available precisely because a single gate CAN compile the union, which is why `gunbc.merge_admission` `receipt_is_admissible` can be the landed ceiling. Here ONE change in one repository breaks a DIFFERENT repository, and the deficit is COVERAGE, not freshness: no gate on either side ever had both repositories in scope, so there is no ordering that helps, no queue to put the pair in -- the pair is not two changes -- and no reviewer position from which the incompatibility is visible. The mitigation #11044 scopes is a claim-channel x surface join over ONE declaration index; it does not reach across the repository boundary, and citing it as coverage here would be citing a wall over the wrong population. ",

    "DISTINCT FROM A BREAKING-CHANGE-TO-A-PUBLISHED-API CLASS. Nothing here is versioned or published. B pins A's MAIN BRANCH, so there is no release boundary that was skipped, no compatibility promise that was broken, and no deprecation window that would have helped: a window is a span between two published versions, and the coupling has neither endpoint. A repair framed as \"version the public surface\" answers a different class and would leave this one intact at any cadence. ",

    "DISTINCT FROM THE STALE-MERGE-BASE CLASS (`verdict_stale_at_the_merge_instant`). Both sides were CURRENT: the public gate compiled current public main, and private CI built against public main as of its own run. Nothing was stale and no verdict was about a superseded tree. The union was simply never compiled by anything, at any freshness. ",

    "RUNG FOUND AT: mitigatable. The failure occurs, is discovered by B's own CI after the fact, and is repaired by hand -- eleven sites, mechanically, in gunbc-private#82. Nothing contained it; what contained the harm was that the repair was mechanical. ",

    "CEILING: MECHANICALLY PREVENTABLE, and the argument for stopping there rather than claiming more. The question is decidable: whether A's change breaks B is answered by compiling A+B, which is a finite compilation of a closure both repositories already name, so this is not the undecidable column and the ceiling is not capped by decidability. It is capped by the STATE remaining writable. A gate in A that compiles B's closure exposes and blocks the class reliably, but the invalid state -- a merge admitted on a narrower closure -- stays perfectly authorable: it is restored by a workflow edit, by a dependent repository nobody enrolled, or by the gate being skipped, and safety depends on that gate executing and staying enrolled. That is the definition of rung 2 (DESIGN section 4b). STRUCTURAL GUARANTEE IS NOT CLAIMED, and the reason is worth stating because the tempting argument is wrong in an instructive way: what stands between here and rung 3 is not a missing proof but a COST -- compiling B's closure on every A change roughly doubles A's gate cost on a fleet already at capacity, and the job roster is closed to growth for exactly that reason. A cost question is not a decidability question, and answering \"we cannot afford it\" with \"therefore it is the ceiling\" is the rung inflation section 4b(1) forbids, in its cheapest form. ",

    "NEXT TRIGGER, NAMED AS THE CAPABILITY AND NOT AS AN ARTIFACT: a gate whose compiled closure includes EVERY repository that builds against this one, with the dependent set DISCOVERED rather than hand-listed -- sufficient to refuse a merge whose compiled closure is a strict subset of the closure its result governs. The discovery half is the load-bearing half. A trigger reading \"add gunbc-private to the public workflow\" is strictly narrower than the capability: it would be SATISFIED by one workflow edit while a third dependent repository stayed invisible, and the class would then be retired while still live -- a drop retired by something other than its trigger (DESIGN section 4b(3)). The grain test the trigger must pass: the loss is over a SET of dependents, so the trigger must be over that set, not over one member of it. ",
  ],

  evidence: [],
}
